WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.930
You helped a friend; they reached into their pocket and asked, "What do I owe you?" Nothing was stolen, nobody was deceived — and yet something broke.

2
00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:13.540
Why do some things turn into something else the moment a price tag touches them?

3
00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:31.310
About five thousand years ago, in one of the temple storerooms of southern Mesopotamia, a scribe pressed the tip of a reed into soft clay and left behind a handful of marks.

4
00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:35.920
What he wrote was not a poem, not a prayer, not a royal inscription.

5
00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:37.970
It was a receipt.

6
00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:43.860
Who had taken how much barley, how much of it would be returned, in which season it would be paid back.

7
00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:50.710
The great majority of humanity's oldest written texts are documents of exactly this kind: who owes what to whom.

8
00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:54.520
Writing was invented not for literature but for bookkeeping.

9
00:00:54.520 --> 00:01:03.450
That fact on its own is worth stopping over, because it lets us understand the story of money the way it actually unfolded rather than the way we are usually told it did.

10
00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:06.780
The story taught in school went like this.

11
00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.510
First came barter.

12
00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:13.920
The shoemaker wanted wheat, the farmer wanted shoes, the two met and traded.

13
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:21.570
But if the farmer happened not to want shoes that day, everything seized up, and money was invented to break the deadlock.

14
00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:25.380
A clean, logical, memorable story.

15
00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:35.750
Its only problem is this: in nearly two centuries of fieldwork, anthropologists have never once come across an economy that ran purely on barter, with no money in it at all.

16
00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:38.760
What they came across was something different.

17
00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:45.930
In small communities people are constantly giving one another things, but they do not take back an equivalent on the spot each time.

18
00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:51.660
The man who helps his neighbour receives nothing in return; he keeps what he is owed in his head.

19
00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:55.470
In other words, before barter there was debt.

20
00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:57.520
There was record.

21
00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.410
There was memory.

22
00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:05.460
The problem money solved was not, at bottom, the awkwardness of swapping goods.

23
00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.310
It was the limit of memory.

24
00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:12.440
In a village you can hold in your mind who owes what to whom.

25
00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:14.490
In a city you cannot.

26
00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:26.620
In a place where thousands of people live side by side, people who have never met, people doing entirely different kinds of work, the only way to keep track of mutual obligations is to measure them and write them down.

27
00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:37.950
In Mesopotamia the name of that measure was the shekel, and at first the shekel was not a coin but a weight: roughly one hundred and eighty grains of barley, about eight grams of silver in today's terms.

28
00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:42.400
Notice that the unit of measurement was itself defined by way of something edible.

29
00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:50.370
Barley was a good you could eat; the shekel was an abstract number that set barley, silver, labour and time along one and the same ruler.

30
00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:55.140
That ruler reached into unexpected places with remarkable speed.

31
00:02:55.140 --> 00:03:04.870
In the stone law code of the Babylonian king Hammurabi, erected some three thousand eight hundred years ago, a physician's fee for surgery is written out in silver shekels.

32
00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:13.480
But the price of the very same operation varies with the patient: one sum for a free man, less for a commoner, less still for a slave.

33
00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:20.650
Which is to say that only a few centuries after inventing the measuring instrument, humanity had already turned it on human beings.

34
00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:28.460
The ruler was not content to compare barley with silver; it also wrote down what saving a life would cost, according to whose life it was.

35
00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:33.870
Coined money, in fact, is a detail that appears midway through this long story.

36
00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:41.520
The first coins in the sense we know today were struck about twenty-six hundred years ago in western Anatolia, in the Kingdom of Lydia.

37
00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:51.890
The Lydians took electrum, the natural alloy of gold and silver carried down in the silt of the Pactolus River, broke it into small pieces, and stamped each one with the seal of the kingdom.

38
00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:56.500
Under King Croesus, gold and silver were fixed to separate standards.

39
00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:01.750
The invention here was not the metal; metal had been in use for thousands of years.

40
00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:04.280
The invention was the stamp.

41
00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:10.650
The stamp said: you do not have to weigh this every time, I vouch for how much this piece is worth.

42
00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:15.740
So the first great invention of money was the transfer of trust into an object.

43
00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:24.510
Aristotle, roughly two centuries after these coins began to circulate in the Aegean world, put the matter with a clarity that has arguably never been bettered.

44
00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:31.360
As he saw it, the precondition for being able to exchange a house for a bed is that the two somehow become comparable.

45
00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:36.930
Yet a house and a bed are by their nature incomparable; they share no common substance.

46
00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:42.180
Money is precisely the instrument that abolishes this incomparability.

47
00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:52.230
Aristotle is careful to stress that money arises not from nature but from agreement: money is a token people have settled on between themselves, a product of law.

48
00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:57.320
Its value comes not from the metal inside it but from the promise we have made about it.

49
00:04:57.320 --> 00:05:01.930
And it is right here that a crack opens, one Aristotle himself noticed.

50
00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:05.900
A metre measures length, but it cannot be accumulated.

51
00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:10.030
A scale measures weight, but nobody stockpiles scales.

52
00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:15.600
Money, though, is both a measure and something that can be hoarded, carried, multiplied.

53
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.370
The instrument invented for measuring is at the same time an object of desire.

54
00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:26.580
Imagine a ruler that can stand in for everything it measures, and on top of that, people want the ruler.

55
00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:30.070
This does not happen in engineering.

56
00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:35.480
In economics it does, because what money measures is not distance but desire.

57
00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:38.810
And the ruler of desire alters the thing it measures.

58
00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:47.580
In seventeen eighty-five, in Königsberg, a professor of philosophy who had scarcely travelled beyond the city of his birth published a slim book.

59
00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:55.870
In that text, known as the Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals, Immanuel Kant was trying to ask where the worth of human conduct comes from.

60
00:05:55.870 --> 00:06:04.160
Halfway through the book he draws a distinction that reads almost like an aside, and yet that distinction laid the floor of the next two centuries of moral argument.

61
00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:10.050
Kant says: if a thing has a price, then something equivalent can be put in its place.

62
00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:13.380
Price is the word that expresses exactly this.

63
00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:19.430
If I can buy a thing for one hundred lira, that means the thing and the hundred lira are interchangeable for me.

64
00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:22.600
Substitutability just is price.

65
00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:30.250
Kant then defines a second category: things that stand above all price and therefore admit of no equivalent whatsoever.

66
00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:33.580
These do not have a price; they have dignity.

67
00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:40.910
The concept is not, in Kant, an expression of emotional loftiness; it carries an entirely technical meaning.

68
00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:45.040
What has dignity is what nothing else can be put in the place of.

69
00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:49.970
And for Kant the human being falls precisely into this second category.

70
00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:53.780
A person is always an end, never merely a means.

71
00:06:53.780 --> 00:07:00.950
Because the moment you use a person merely as a means, you have assumed that someone else could be slotted in to do the same job.

72
00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:04.920
That assumption is itself a way of pricing a human being.

73
00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:08.570
Kant adds that price itself comes in two kinds.

74
00:07:08.570 --> 00:07:14.140
On one side there is market price: the price of things that answer to a need.

75
00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:17.790
Flour, coal, carpentry.

76
00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:28.320
On the other side there are things that correspond to no need at all and yet have worth because people take pleasure in them; Kant calls this fancy price, the price of feeling.

77
00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:32.130
Jokes, good conversation, imagination.

78
00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:38.660
A display of wit has a price too, on his account, just not one that trades on any market.

79
00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:43.430
Dignity, though, differs from both of these categorically, not by degree.

80
00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:52.520
Saying "extremely expensive" does not bring you any closer to saying "beyond price." They are two separate languages.

81
00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:58.890
Now let us look at where this abstract distinction touches down, because the place it touches is very concrete.

82
00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:05.100
An engineer deciding today whether a new barrier should be built along a mountain road looks at a table.

83
00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:11.630
In that table there is the cost of the barrier, and there is the estimated number of deaths the barrier would prevent.

84
00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:17.040
To reach a decision, the engineer has to convert those two columns into a single unit.

85
00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:21.170
Which means writing down a numerical equivalent for a human life.

86
00:08:21.170 --> 00:08:25.300
This is not a malicious calculation; it is an unavoidable one.

87
00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:34.710
In the United States, federal agencies use a figure for this purpose called the value of a statistical life, which in recent years has settled above ten million dollars.

88
00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:40.920
In Turkey a similar logic operates when investments in road safety and workplace safety are justified.

89
00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:44.890
Those who want to ease the tension here take one of two easy exits.

90
00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:50.780
The first is to reject the calculation outright by declaring that no price can be put on a human life.

91
00:08:50.780 --> 00:09:01.150
The stance looks morally noble, but in practice it arrives at this result: the barrier does not get built, because no other way remains of deciding which road gets which barrier.

92
00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:08.960
As long as resources are limited, refusing to calculate does not remove the calculation; it makes it invisible and arbitrary.

93
00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:17.570
The second easy exit is the exact opposite: once the number has been written down the matter is closed, and a human life really is worth that much.

94
00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:22.180
This is not a philosophical error so much as a logical confusion.

95
00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:29.190
Kant's distinction turns out to be surprisingly helpful here, because it lets us name correctly what that number measures.

96
00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:33.480
The value of a statistical life is not the value of a human being.

97
00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:36.970
No one's name is written into that calculation.

98
00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:42.700
What is being measured is how much a society is willing to give up in order to reduce the risk of death.

99
00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:47.630
So what it measures is our preference, not the worth of the person across from us.

100
00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:56.400
The moment we confuse the two, the very same calculator begins to speak an entirely different sentence: this person's life is not worth this cost.

101
00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:02.290
And we are back at those three separate fees on Hammurabi's stone, only with a more elegant table.

102
00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:07.700
The line Kant drew is therefore not about the use of money but about the grammar of money.

103
00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:11.510
Money is a language, and like every language it translates.

104
00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:17.720
The trouble is not that something is lost in translation; something is lost in every translation.

105
00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:22.010
The trouble is that we take the translation to have replaced the original.

106
00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:25.660
So why does this confusion happen so often?

107
00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:30.270
Why does the measure slide so easily into the place of the thing it measures?

108
00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:36.960
Seven centuries before Kant, a thinker in Khorasan had answered that question through the nature of money itself.

109
00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:44.290
Al-Ghazālī was born in Tūs, in the north-east of what is now Iran, and built one of the most brilliant careers of his age.

110
00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:50.340
He taught at the head of the Nizāmiyya madrasa in Baghdad, from the most prestigious chair in the Islamic world.

111
00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:57.190
At the age of thirty-seven he left that chair, left the city, and lived for years in a kind of inward reckoning.

112
00:10:57.190 --> 00:11:05.160
The great work he wrote after that departure is the Iḥyāʾ ʿulūm al-dīn, The Revival of the Religious Sciences.

113
00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:09.610
Its forty books run from worship to ethics, from the heart to commerce.

114
00:11:09.610 --> 00:11:16.940
And in one of those books he takes up a subject one would not expect from a scholar of religion: the question of what money is.

115
00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:20.910
The scene al-Ghazālī sets is taken from everyday life.

116
00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:24.880
Picture a man holding saffron who wants to buy a camel.

117
00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:28.690
The man across from him has a camel and wants saffron.

118
00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:32.020
So how much saffron comes to one camel?

119
00:11:32.020 --> 00:11:39.190
This is the same question as Aristotle's question about incomparability, but al-Ghazālī resolves it with a different image.

120
00:11:39.190 --> 00:11:44.920
Something is needed to judge between the saffron and the camel, he says; a judge.

121
00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:49.050
Gold and silver are those judges that give rulings among goods.

122
00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:53.660
Their value comes not from themselves but from the things they rule between.

123
00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:56.350
Then he makes the famous comparison.

124
00:11:56.350 --> 00:12:01.920
Money, he says, resembles a mirror that has no colour of its own yet shows every colour.

125
00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:07.010
It has no form in itself; it takes the form of whatever is set before it.

126
00:12:07.010 --> 00:12:11.300
The elegance of the comparison lies in the criticism folded inside it.

127
00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:14.310
The function of a mirror is to reflect.

128
00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:19.880
If you look into a mirror and begin to admire the mirror itself, the mirror has lost its function.

129
00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:28.650
Al-Ghazālī's entire warning about money stands inside that single image: the measure stops measuring the moment it takes the place of what it measures.

130
00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:32.140
This is why he objects openly to two things.

131
00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:36.110
The first is hoarding money and withdrawing it from circulation.

132
00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:39.920
By his logic this is like throwing the judge in prison.

133
00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:45.970
A judge exists to move among people and to give rulings; gold locked in a chest serves no one.

134
00:12:45.970 --> 00:12:48.820
Note that this is not a hymn to poverty.

135
00:12:48.820 --> 00:13:00.950
The world al-Ghazālī lived in was an intensely mobile commercial world stretching from Baghdad to Samarkand, from Cairo to Andalusia: caravans, letters of credit, partnerships, promissory notes.

136
00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:05.400
He was writing from inside that world and he regarded trade as legitimate.

137
00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:12.090
What he objected to was not buying and selling but the instrument of buying and selling becoming an end in itself.

138
00:13:12.090 --> 00:13:17.660
His second objection is to interest, and his reasoning there is conceptual rather than legal.

139
00:13:17.660 --> 00:13:24.350
For al-Ghazālī, exchanging money for money with an increase turns the judge into one of the parties to the case.

140
00:13:24.350 --> 00:13:29.920
The measuring instrument mixes into the crowd it was measuring and starts turning a profit on its own account.

141
00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:36.130
Picture a scale that takes a cut of every weighing; you can no longer trust a single number that scale gives you.

142
00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:44.100
You are not obliged to accept this line of reasoning, and the centuries-long argument about the economic function of interest still stands where it stood.

143
00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:52.070
But it is clear where al-Ghazālī's worry lands: when the means turns into the end, the system's capacity to measure breaks down.

144
00:13:52.070 --> 00:13:59.400
And the calmest, most unsettling feature of his diagnosis is this: he is not saying that people are wicked.

145
00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:02.570
He complains about no one's intentions.

146
00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:05.420
What he is describing is structural.

147
00:14:05.420 --> 00:14:14.830
Once you invent an instrument that can measure everything, that instrument becomes an object of desire in its own right, because from then on the road to every desire runs through it.

148
00:14:14.830 --> 00:14:18.000
Ill will is not required, only time.

149
00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:25.810
If a mirror reflects everything for long enough, at some point people start looking for things to reflect just so they can look at the mirror.

150
00:14:25.810 --> 00:14:29.460
So what happens when this reversal takes place?

151
00:14:29.460 --> 00:14:35.190
Answering that, al-Ghazālī had two examples to hand: hoarding and interest.

152
00:14:35.190 --> 00:14:38.680
We now have far more material to work with.

153
00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:48.250
Because over the past half-century price has seeped into areas neither al-Ghazālī nor Kant ever contemplated, and we no longer have to guess what happens where it seeps in.

154
00:14:48.250 --> 00:14:50.300
We can measure it.

155
00:14:50.300 --> 00:15:00.030
In Washington, before important sessions of the United States Congress, some of the people queuing outside the buildings are not citizens who have come to watch the proceedings.

156
00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:05.760
They are people paid by the hour who will hand their place over to a lobbyist or a lawyer who is paying them.

157
00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:10.050
There are companies set up for this work, and there are price lists.

158
00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:13.700
The place in line has become a saleable good.

159
00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:23.590
Most people feel the discomfort here at once, but naming its cause is hard, because on the face of it nobody has been harmed: the person standing in line is content with the money,

160
00:15:23.590 --> 00:15:27.560
and the person buying the place is content with the seat.

161
00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:33.610
In his book What Money Can't Buy, Michael Sandel shows that this discomfort has two distinct sources.

162
00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:35.980
The first is inequality.

163
00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:42.670
Once something becomes purchasable, reaching it depends no longer on your relationship to the thing but on your wallet.

164
00:15:42.670 --> 00:15:51.440
The fairness of queuing lies in everyone's time flowing at the same rate; a place in line skipped with money separates those who have time from those who have money.

165
00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:54.610
The second and deeper objection is corruption.

166
00:15:54.610 --> 00:16:00.820
Some things, when sold, are not merely distributed unfairly; they turn into something else.

167
00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:05.270
And this second objection is the everyday counterpart of Kant's distinction.

168
00:16:05.270 --> 00:16:08.600
The most striking example of it is the apology.

169
00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:19.450
In China and in many other countries there are services that apologise on a client's behalf, that write letters of apology, that will even go in person to express remorse.

170
00:16:19.450 --> 00:16:29.340
Logically it looks flawless: the injured party wants to hear an apology, the offending party wants an apology conveyed, and the intermediary does it more effectively.

171
00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:35.390
But the moment the injured party learns that the apology was purchased, they are left with nothing at all.

172
00:16:35.390 --> 00:16:40.160
Because the worth of an apology came precisely from the difficulty of saying it.

173
00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:44.770
When you buy the difficulty, what you have bought is no longer an apology.

174
00:16:44.770 --> 00:16:49.060
Price does not take possession of the good; it evaporates the good.

175
00:16:49.060 --> 00:16:52.710
There are measured examples of this evaporation.

176
00:16:52.710 --> 00:17:00.200
In Israel, a group of economists watched what happened at day-care centres that began fining parents who picked their children up late.

177
00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:04.010
The expectation was that the fine would reduce lateness.

178
00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:07.180
Lateness rose, and markedly so.

179
00:17:07.180 --> 00:17:15.950
The reason was this: before the fine, being late was a way of keeping a teacher from going home, a wrong done to her; what the parent felt was shame.

180
00:17:15.950 --> 00:17:21.360
Once the fine was introduced, being late turned into a service with a stated price.

181
00:17:21.360 --> 00:17:25.970
The parent was no longer wronging anyone; they were buying overtime.

182
00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:36.500
And after the fine was withdrawn, lateness did not fall back to its former level, because the shared understanding of what kind of relationship this was had been broken once and for all.

183
00:17:36.500 --> 00:17:44.150
The British social policy researcher Richard Titmuss described a similar mechanism more than forty years ago while studying blood donation.

184
00:17:44.150 --> 00:17:49.560
When payment was offered for giving blood, a portion of the voluntary donors withdrew.

185
00:17:49.560 --> 00:18:00.410
For them, giving blood was a kindness done to a stranger; once a fee entered the picture that kindness turned into a poorly paid job, and people did not want to be someone who sells their own blood.

186
00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:06.620
Economists argued over Titmuss's thesis for a long while, and some of his findings were retested.

187
00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:12.830
But the argument itself was forced to concede one thing: an incentive sometimes subtracts rather than adds.

188
00:18:12.830 --> 00:18:17.280
And then there is the heaviest end of this argument: the kidney.

189
00:18:17.280 --> 00:18:28.130
A healthy person can live with one kidney, tens of thousands of people around the world die waiting their turn on dialysis, and the number of patients waiting is many times the number of kidneys donated.

190
00:18:28.130 --> 00:18:36.900
Since nineteen eighty-eight, Iran has been the only country to legally permit payment to kidney donors, and waiting lists there have been reported to shorten.

191
00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:47.270
Against that, research in the same country has found that the overwhelming majority of donors are poor people in debt, and that many of them report regret and health problems years after the payment.

192
00:18:47.270 --> 00:18:51.880
What faces us here is not an abstract question of philosophy.

193
00:18:51.880 --> 00:19:00.490
On one side are patients dying, on the other are people with no way out but to sell their bodies, and no answer comes away with clean hands.

194
00:19:00.490 --> 00:19:04.140
The genuinely dark point is perhaps this.

195
00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:09.870
When price enters a domain, it pushes out all the other languages that used to do the work there.

196
00:19:09.870 --> 00:19:21.200
Once you have learned to ask "how much?", it is no longer as easy to ask "is this right?", "does this person deserve it?", "what is owed to me here?", because those questions do not yield a measurable answer,

197
00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:25.330
and unmeasurable answers look feeble standing next to measurable ones.

198
00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:36.020
For centuries al-Ghazālī's mirror has kept finding new surfaces to reflect: the queue, the apology, blood, organs, attention, friendship, sleep.

199
00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:42.390
On each new surface it works better, and on each new surface it subtracts something from what it reflects.

200
00:19:42.390 --> 00:19:50.520
In one of his plays, Oscar Wilde defined the cynic as a man who knows the price of everything and the value of nothing.

201
00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:53.850
The line is usually read as an insult.

202
00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:57.340
In fact there is a description of a loss inside it.

203
00:19:57.340 --> 00:20:03.710
The person who knows the price of everything is not ignorant; on the contrary, he knows a great deal.

204
00:20:03.710 --> 00:20:14.080
What he has lost is not knowledge but the capacity to draw a distinction: the capacity to tell apart the cases where something else can be put in the place of what he holds from the cases where it cannot.

205
00:20:14.080 --> 00:20:20.610
When that distinction disappears, what remains is not a miscalculation but a world reduced to a single ruler.

206
00:20:20.610 --> 00:20:27.620
And in a world with one ruler there is nothing that is beyond price; there are only things whose price has not yet been asked.

207
00:20:27.620 --> 00:20:31.750
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

208
00:20:31.750 --> 00:20:35.400
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
